    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Offer Evaluation\n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Google Offer (L6) | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **$5k drop**; band cap is $271,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | Target increases by $11,000 |\n| **Annualized Equity** | ~$250,000 | $270,000 ($1.08M / 4 yrs) | Straight-line average |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | N/A | $0 | Standard L6 sign-ons are $50k\u2013$100k+ |\n| **Year 1 Target TC (Annualized)** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | +$26,000/yr over current run-rate |\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Google Equity Dynamic: Front-Loading & Refreshes\n\nGoogle uses a **front-loaded Google Stock Unit (GSU) vesting schedule** (typically 38% Year 1, 32% Year 2, 20% Year 3, 10% Year 4):\n\n* **Year 1:** $235k Base + $47k Bonus + $410.4k Stock (38%) = **~$692,400**\n* **Year 2:** $235k Base + $47k Bonus + $345.6k Stock (32%) = **~$627,600**\n* **Year 3:** $235k Base + $47k Bonus + $216.0k Stock (20%) + *Refresher 1*\n* **Year 4:** $235k Base + $47k Bonus + $108.0k Stock (10%) + *Refreshers 1\u20133*\n\n#### What this means:\n* **The upside:** Year 1 and Year 2 cash flow will be substantially higher than Meta ($692k vs. $526k in Year 1).\n* **The cliff:** In Years 3 and 4, the initial grant drops sharply. You will rely heavily on annual equity refreshers (which Google grants during annual reviews) to keep your compensation above $550k\u2013$600k. \n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**Negotiate.** \n\nYou have high leverage: you were headhunted, you are happy in your current role, and Google's offer asks you to take a slight cut in base salary with **no sign-on bonus** to offset leaving Meta.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Negotiation Counter-Proposal\n\nAsk for improvements across three areas:\n\n```\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502  Target Counter Package:                               \u2502\n\u2502  \u2022 Base Salary:    $255,000                            \u2502\n\u2502  \u2022 Sign-On Bonus:  $75,000 \u2013 $100,000                  \u2502\n\u2502  \u2022 Total Equity:   $1,200,000 \u2013 $1,300,000 / 4 years   \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\n\n#### 1. Base Salary: Ask for $255,000\n* **Rationale:** The current offer of $235,000 is lower than your current $240,000 base. With the posted salary band going up to $271,000, asking for $255k is well within the band and ensures you do not take a step back in fixed cash.\n\n#### 2. Sign-On Bonus: Ask for $75,000 \u2013 $100,000\n* **Rationale:** You are leaving ~$200,000 in unvested Meta RSUs over the next 12 months. A sign-on bonus is standard for L6 external hires and is the easiest lever for a recruiter to bridge transition costs without breaking compensation equity bands.\n\n#### 3. Equity Grant: Ask for $1,250,000 (over 4 years)\n* **Rationale:** An increase from $1.08M to $1.25M pushes your annualized stock to ~$312k/yr. This brings your steady-state baseline well above Meta even when the initial front-loading tapers in Years 3 and 4.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Script to Use with the Recruiter\n\n> *\"Thank you for extending this offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m excited about the scope of the role. \n> \n> Because I wasn't actively looking and have strong momentum and unvested equity at Meta, compensation is a critical factor in making this move. \n> \n> Looking at the numbers, the base of $235,000 is slightly below my current $240,000 base. Additionally, walking away from Meta means leaving ~$200,000 of RSUs vesting over the next year. \n> \n> If we can adjust the **base to $255,000**, include a **$100,000 sign-on bonus** to offset the transition, and increase the **equity grant to $1.25M over 4 years**, this becomes an easy decision and I would be ready to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 6. Due Diligence Questions to Ask\n1. **Confirm the exact vesting schedule:** Confirm whether this grant follows the standard 38%/32%/20%/10% schedule with monthly or quarterly vesting.\n2. **First refresher eligibility:** Ask when you will be eligible for your first annual refresher grant cycle (e.g., in your first annual review cycle or after a full 12 months).",
