    "message": {
     "content": "### The Short Answer\n\n**Yes, you should take it**\u2014provided you are genuinely excited about the role and team at Google. \n\nFinancially, this is an **exceptional, top-of-band Google L6 PM offer**. While they did not give you cash up front as a sign-on bonus, they put the entire $200,000 you asked for directly into your equity grant ($1.08M \u2192 $1.28M). Because Google heavily front-loads equity, this change pays you out significantly faster than a flat sign-on bonus would have.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Numbers: Year-by-Year Breakdown\n\nGoogle's standard vesting schedule vests **38% in Year 1, 32% in Year 2, 20% in Year 3, and 10% in Year 4** (vesting monthly after an initial period).\n\n| Year | Base | Target Bonus (20%) | GSU Vesting (from $1.28M) | Target Total Comp (Excl. Refreshers) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $240,000 | $48,000 | **$486,400** (38%) | **$774,400** |\n| **Year 2** | $240,000 | $48,000 | **$409,600** (32%) | **$697,600** |\n| **Year 3** | $240,000 | $48,000 | **$256,000** (20%) | **$544,000** *(+ Refresher 1)* |\n| **Year 4** | $240,000 | $48,000 | **$128,000** (10%) | **$416,000** *(+ Refreshers 1\u20133)* |\n| **4-Yr Avg** | **$240,000** | **$48,000** | **$320,000/yr** | **$608,000/year** |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why This Completely Solves Your $200,000 \"Walk-Away\" Problem\n\n* **At Meta:** You projected earning **~$526,000** over the next 12 months (which includes that $200,000 in vesting stock).\n* **At Google:** In Year 1, you will earn **~$774,400**.\n\nThat is an immediate **+$248,400 bump in Year 1 alone**. The front-loaded equity structure effectively replaces the $200,000 of unvested Meta stock you are forfeiting within your very first 12 months on the job.\n\nOver the 4-year grant period, your annualized baseline is **$608,000/year** (up +$82,000/year over Meta), even before accounting for annual equity refreshers or stock appreciation.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Final Decision Checklist Before You Sign\n\nSince the financial package is essentially maximized for an L6, your decision should now hinge on these non-compensation factors:\n\n1. **The \"Cold Start\" Cost vs. Established Capital:**\n   * At Meta, you are an established IC6 with trust, cross-functional momentum, and known performance. \n   * At Google, you will spend the first 3\u20136 months building political capital, learning internal tooling, and establishing influence.\n2. **Promotion Velocity (L6 \u2192 L7 vs. IC6 \u2192 IC7):**\n   * Meta generally moves faster on promotions for top performers, but has higher churn and intensity.\n   * Google L6 to L7 is notoriously deliberate and requires significant cross-org consensus. If reaching L7/IC7 quickly is your top priority, evaluate which environment gives you the clearest scope.\n3. **Product Area & Scope:**\n   * Is the Google product tier-1 (core search, ads, cloud, core infra) or a more exploratory bet? Ensure you are entering a high-visibility org with healthy headcount and executive sponsorship.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendation\n\n**Accept the offer.** Google met your compensation floor, matched your base, and structured an equity package that will pay you over **$1.47M across your first two years** ($774k + $698k). It is a clean win on compensation.",
